---
import ToolPage from '../layouts/ToolPage.astro';
import LoadPlanner from '../components/LoadPlanner.astro';
import data from '../data/containers.json';
import { trailerPresets } from '../data/trailers.js';
const hc = data.containers.find((c) => c.id === '40hc')!;
const defaults = {
  cont: { preset: hc.id, L: hc.L, W: hc.W, H: hc.H, doorW: hc.doorW, doorH: hc.doorH, maxWeight: hc.payload },
  priority: false,
  items: [
    { name: 'Carton A', l: 600, w: 400, h: 400, weight: 12, qty: 400, rotation: 'any', stackable: true },
    { name: 'Carton B', l: 500, w: 300, h: 300, weight: 6, qty: 500, rotation: 'upright', stackable: true },
  ],
};
const n = (v: number) => v.toLocaleString('en-US');
---
<ToolPage
  title="Container Loading Calculator (Free 3D Load Plan)"
  description="Free 3D container loading calculator: see how many cartons fit in a 20ft, 40ft or 40ft high cube. Automatic plans, loading steps, carton orientation and hand editing in 3D."
  h1="Container loading calculator"
  name="Container loading calculator"
  lede={`Enter your carton sizes, weights and quantities to see how many fit in a 20', 40', 40' high cube or 45' container, with a 3D plan you can step through. For example, 600 × 400 × 400 mm cartons: <strong>660 fit in a 40' high cube</strong> (inside ${n(hc.L)} × ${n(hc.W)} × ${n(hc.H)} mm) — 30 rows along the length, each row 22 cartons: three columns of six lying 600 mm across plus one column of four standing 600 mm tall.`}
>
  <LoadPlanner mode="container" defaults={defaults} spacePresets={[...data.containers, ...trailerPresets]} presetLabel="Container or trailer" />

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>How the calculator works</h2>
    <p>The planner fills the container from the back wall toward the door. At each step it takes the empty space nearest the back and the floor, and fills its corner with the best block of identical cartons — a set of rows, columns and layers in one orientation. What is left of that space is cut into smaller spaces, and the process repeats until nothing more fits or every carton is loaded. This is the “block building” method used in container loading research (Eley, 2002; Fanslau and Bortfeldt, 2010).</p>
    <p>The planner tries many variations — different ways of scoring blocks and cutting the leftover space, plus seeded random choices — and keeps the plan that loads the most cargo volume. The same inputs always give the same plan, so a shared link shows exactly what you saw.</p>
    <p>Before a plan is shown, an independent check confirms that no two cartons overlap, every carton is inside the container and fits through the door opening in the way it is stowed, “this side up” cartons are not tipped, every carton stands fully on the floor or on cartons below it, nothing is stacked on a carton marked as not stackable, and the payload limit is not exceeded.</p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Any side can face down:</strong> the carton may be stowed in any of its six orientations.</li>
      <li><strong>This side up:</strong> the carton keeps its height vertical; it can only be turned flat (length and width swapped).</li>
      <li><strong>Can carry boxes on top:</strong> untick for fragile cartons or crates that must not be stacked on.</li>
      <li><strong>Load in list order:</strong> by default the planner loads whatever combination uses the most space. Tick this when the first box type must go in before the others.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>Choosing carton orientation</h2>
    <p>Every carton can sit in six positions: one of its three sides faces up, and its length either runs along the container (toward the door) or across it. Under <strong>Orientation</strong> you choose how much the planner may decide:</p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Automatic:</strong> the planner tries every allowed position for each block and keeps the mix that loads the most. With “this side up” it only turns cartons flat.</li>
      <li><strong>Choose the positions allowed:</strong> tap the drawings to allow or block each of the six positions — for example, allow only “height up” and “length up” for cartons that must not lie on their side. Leave one position ticked to fix it completely.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The table under the plan shows which positions were used and how many cartons are in each. For the 600 × 400 × 400 mm carton above, fixing it in one position (400 mm up, 600 mm along) gives 20 × 5 × 6 = <strong>600 cartons</strong> in a 40' high cube, while letting the planner mix positions gives 660.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>Fully automatic or semi-automatic loading</h2>
    <p><strong>Fully automatic</strong> leaves everything to the planner: the order of box types, where each block goes and which way each carton faces.</p>
    <p><strong>Semi-automatic</strong> lets you set the loading sequence the way your warehouse will actually load, and the planner works out the exact position of every carton in each step. Each step has:</p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Box type and how many:</strong> leave the quantity empty to load all that are left of that type.</li>
      <li><strong>Position:</strong> automatic (best of the positions you allow for that box) or one fixed position for this step only.</li>
      <li><strong>Start a new section:</strong> the step begins in front of everything already loaded, so later cargo is not tucked into gaps behind it. Use this to keep orders, destinations or stops together.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Steps run in order from the back wall toward the door, and you can move or delete them. With “Then load whatever is left automatically” ticked, the planner finishes with the cartons no step took. The step table shows how many cartons each step asked for and how many it loaded, and the loading-step slider under the 3D view tells you which step each carton belongs to. Every semi-automatic plan passes the same independent check as an automatic one, plus checks that each step used only its own box type, kept its fixed position, and that new sections start in front of earlier cargo.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>Editing the plan by hand</h2>
    <p>Under the 3D view, <strong>Edit this plan by hand</strong> turns any plan into one you can adjust, and <strong>Load by hand from empty</strong> starts with an empty container. Tap a box in the 3D view (or step through the boxes with ‹ ›) to select it, then:</p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Turn</strong> it into another of its allowed positions, where it stands.</li>
      <li><strong>Push</strong> it toward the back wall, the door, left, right, up or down: it slides until it meets a wall or another box.</li>
      <li><strong>Pick a new spot:</strong> green boxes in the 3D view show every free spot where it fits; tap one.</li>
      <li><strong>Remove</strong> it. Boxes standing on it come out with it, and all of them are listed under “Not loaded”.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Boxes under “Not loaded” can be placed one at a time: choose a position or leave it automatic, then tap a green spot or use the next free spot (nearest the back wall and the floor). <strong>Fill the rest automatically</strong> keeps every box you placed and loads what is left around them. Undo and Redo step through your changes, and the share link keeps your hand edits.</p>
    <p>Every change is checked before it is made, with the same rules as an automatic plan: a box cannot overlap another, must stand fully on the floor or on boxes below, cannot go on a box marked as not stackable, must use a position it is allowed, and must pass the door the way it is stowed. A box with others standing on it cannot be moved or turned until they are moved. If a change would break a rule, the planner says why instead of making it. Changing the boxes or the container afterwards starts again from an automatic plan.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>Container sizes used in the presets</h2>
    <p>Inside dimensions, door openings and payloads below are Hapag-Lloyd's published figures. Hapag-Lloyd notes that its figures are examples and that containers vary by manufacturer, so check your own unit's data plate or booking when the fit is tight, and pick “Custom size” to enter it.</p>
    <div class="tablebox">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Container</th><th class="num">Inside L × W × H (mm)</th><th class="num">Door W × H (mm)</th><th class="num">Max payload (kg)</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.containers.map((c) => <tr><td><a href={c.url} rel="noopener">{c.name}</a></td><td class="num">{n(c.L)} × {n(c.W)} × {n(c.H)}</td><td class="num">{n(c.doorW)} × {n(c.doorH)}</td><td class="num">{n(c.payload)}</td></tr>)}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="small">Source: Hapag-Lloyd container specification pages (linked), checked {data.source.checked}. More detail on <a href="/shipping-container-dimensions/">shipping container dimensions</a>; pallet footprints and heights on <a href="/pallet-sizes/">standard pallet sizes</a>. To plan the cartons on each pallet first, use the <a href="/pallet-loading-calculator/">pallet loading calculator</a>; for whole pallets, see <a href="/how-many-pallets-fit-in-a-container/">how many pallets fit in a container</a>. For the total volume of your cartons in cubic metres or cubic feet, use the <a href="/cbm-calculator/">CBM calculator</a>.</p>
    <p>The list also has three 53' dry vans with the interior sizes Hyundai Translead publishes ({trailerPresets.map((t) => t.name.replace("53' dry van, Hyundai Translead ", '')).join(', ')}). Their pages give no door opening or payload, so those fields stay empty (no door check, no weight limit) until you enter your own. Pallet counts for these trailers are on <a href="/how-many-pallets-fit-in-a-53-foot-trailer/">how many pallets fit in a 53 ft trailer</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>Getting a plan that works on the dock</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li>Measure cartons as packed, at the widest point. Bulging cartons are often a few millimetres bigger than the printed size, and a few millimetres per carton add up over 30 rows.</li>
      <li>Leave room for dunnage, airbags and the door: a plan that uses every millimetre is a plan that will not close.</li>
      <li>The payload limit is the most the container may carry, but road weight limits where the container travels can be lower. Check with your trucker for the inland leg.</li>
      <li>Put heavy cartons low and spread the weight along the floor. The planner shows the centre of gravity of the cargo along the length so you can see if the load leans toward one end.</li>
      <li>The <a href="https://www.imo.org/en/ourwork/safety/pages/ctu-code.aspx" rel="noopener">IMO/ILO/UNECE Code of Practice for Packing of Cargo Transport Units (CTU Code)</a> is the international guidance on packing, securing and weight distribution in containers.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>Related</h2>
    <div class="chips">
      <a href="/shipping-container-dimensions/">Shipping container dimensions</a>
      <a href="/pallet-loading-calculator/">Pallet loading calculator</a>
      <a href="/how-many-pallets-fit-in-a-container/">Pallets in a container</a>
      <a href="/how-many-pallets-fit-in-a-53-foot-trailer/">Pallets in a 53 ft trailer</a>
      <a href="/cbm-calculator/">CBM calculator</a>
      <a href="/dimensional-weight-calculator/">Dimensional weight calculator</a>
      <a href="/pallet-sizes/">Standard pallet sizes</a>
      <a href="/how-we-calculate/">How we calculate</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</ToolPage>
